import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.dWYrTn1b.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Changelog","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"development/changelog.md","filePath":"development/changelog.md"}'),n={name:"development/changelog.md"};function r(i,e,d,c,l,h){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="changelog" tabindex="-1">Changelog <a class="header-anchor" href="#changelog" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Changelog&quot;">​</a></h1><p>All notable changes to this project are documented here.</p><h2 id="unreleased" tabindex="-1">[Unreleased] <a class="header-anchor" href="#unreleased" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;[Unreleased]&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="changed" tabindex="-1">Changed <a class="header-anchor" href="#changed" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Changed&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>The demo program and the component library its examples use live in <code>examples/</code> (<code>examples/demo</code>, <code>examples/library</code>) instead of among the test fixtures. The tests, the getting-started guide and the README read the same four files, so the guide&#39;s copy can no longer drift from the tests&#39; (it had: the tests still stated <code>is_static</code> on the demo&#39;s obstacles).</li><li>The documentation is a VitePress site in <code>docs/</code>, laid out as a guide (Introduction, Basics, Advanced), the reference specification, and the development notes, after Lucide&#39;s documentation. The guide is new: a first program from source to a run, one page per file and per concern, and the deeper pages on parts, drive types, sensors, validation, judging a run and the two simulators, with the language highlighted. Every example in it compiles, and the getting-started run was carried out as written. The generated vocabulary page also stops splitting a row at a choice&#39;s <code>|</code>. The site is published inside the project site at <a href="https://mit-cdfg.github.io/robotdsl-site/docs/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://mit-cdfg.github.io/robotdsl-site/docs/</a>, in the site&#39;s type and colour, and the site&#39;s own page is its home.</li><li>A scene entity stays in place unless the program says otherwise: <code>is_static</code> defaults to <code>true</code>, and both targets refuse an entity declared movable (<code>is_static = false</code>), since an entity states no mass to move it with. The Gazebo target gave a movable entity SDF&#39;s default inertial, 1 kg and a unit inertia, and a <code>Marker</code> without the flag, having no collision, fell through the floor; the evaluator also judges contact against each entity&#39;s declared pose. All 145 entities in robotdsl-experiment&#39;s 53 worlds already declare <code>is_static = true</code>, so their output does not change.</li></ul><h3 id="fixed" tabindex="-1">Fixed <a class="header-anchor" href="#fixed" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Fixed&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>The <code>ros-mujoco-nav2</code> target builds the world the program states. The node ran every world with MuJoCo&#39;s own gravity and a ground plane, and held every entity in place: a world in free fall without a floor ran as the demo room, and a movable obstacle became a fixed one. The scene now carries the world&#39;s gravity and whether it has a ground plane; an entity declared movable is refused, as is a base environment other than <code>&quot;empty&quot;</code>.</li><li>The MuJoCo node&#39;s IMU reads linear acceleration. MuJoCo fills body accelerations only when a model has acceleration sensors, and the node computes its sensors itself, so a body pushed at 3 m/s^2 read zero while gravity was added by hand. The node now fills them before reading, and a container check reads an IMU at rest and pushed against the known answer.</li><li>A ground contact a part declares under <code>contacts</code> is simulated: both targets give it a sphere collision on a fixed link of its own that slides like a caster. The statics rule counted such a skid as a support while neither simulator had it, so a two-wheel base on a molded skid passed the rule and then rested tipped 10 degrees onto its chassis in Gazebo and MuJoCo alike. The motion checks now also require every base to rest level.</li><li>The <code>ros-mujoco-nav2</code> target refuses a part that declares no collision box. The robot description collides such a part through its Collada visual mesh, which MuJoCo cannot read, so the program compiled and then stopped at start-up. In the format-1 catalog 36 of 73 chassis and 7 of 9 sensors declare no collision box.</li></ul><h3 id="added" tabindex="-1">Added <a class="header-anchor" href="#added" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Added&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>Add architecture, language, and developer onboarding documentation.</li><li>Add the <code>mobiledsl</code> package skeleton, the import-boundary tests that enforce the documented dependency direction, and CI.</li><li>Add the shared diagnostic, span, and source-map types (<code>support/</code>), taken from MobileDSL-Compiler.</li><li>Add the frontend for the four source forms (<code>.core</code>, <code>.world</code>, <code>.task</code>, <code>.eval</code>): one grammar with a start rule per form, a source-anchored syntax tree, and syntax diagnostics. Compared with the previous compiler, component types, task node types and check names are ordinary names resolved later instead of grammar keywords; poses and vectors are tuples instead of strings; references are <code>@world/goal</code> instead of <code>&quot;@world/goal&quot;</code>; <code>#</code> comments are gone.</li><li>Render diagnostics in the terminal the way rustc and ruff do: a heading with the code, an excerpt with the spans underlined, <code>help</code> and <code>note</code> lines, and color when writing to a terminal.</li><li>Add the component library loader: <code>library.yaml</code> plus <code>parts/&lt;id&gt;/component.yaml</code>, read as spanned YAML so that every problem points into the library file. Compared with the previous compiler&#39;s <code>part.yaml</code>: <code>model</code> is <code>visual</code>, <code>geometry</code> is <code>wheel</code>, the joint moves into the connector that creates it, and catalog images are no longer checked by the compiler.</li><li>Add the standard vocabulary (<code>semantic/model/vocabulary.py</code>): the component types, entity types, task nodes and checks a program may name, with their parameters. The specification page is generated from it. The task vocabulary names what the robot must do (<code>Navigate</code>, <code>Spin</code>, <code>Wait</code>, ...) and nothing about how a navigation stack does it.</li><li>Add lowering (<code>semantic/lower.py</code>): resolve the four syntax trees against the vocabulary and the loaded libraries into one typed program, reporting every unknown name, ill-typed argument, unresolved reference and structural problem in one pass, with suggestions for near misses.</li><li>Add analysis (<code>semantic/analysis/</code>): mate each component onto the chassis connector its glue names, and derive placements, wheel contacts, mass and centre of mass, the footprint from declared collision shapes, drive facts, sensors, goal poses and task use. A glue that matches no connector is a fact (<code>unplaced</code>), not a guess.</li><li>Add validation (<code>validation/</code>): rules that answer pass, fail, unknown or not applicable over the facts, with evidence and findings that point at the program. Standard rules cover glue-to-connector matching, connector occupancy, wheel mounts and classes, horizontal axles, a common contact plane, footprint coverage, and whether the task&#39;s Spin and Strafe suit the base. The rule table is rendered into the specification.</li><li>Add the driver and the <code>mobiledsl compile</code> command: the four files as arguments, <code>--library</code> directories, <code>--check</code>, <code>--json</code>, <code>--warnings-as-errors</code> and <code>--color</code>. Diagnostics and a summary go to stderr, colored on a terminal; the structured result goes to stdout. No target exists yet, so <code>--target</code> reports that.</li><li>Specify the grammar, the vocabulary, the component library format, the validation rules, the diagnostic contract, and the output contract.</li><li>Add the target interface (<code>targets/api.py</code>): a target turns the program and its facts into exact file bytes plus copies of library assets, declares every stand-in it makes as an approximation, and refuses what it cannot realize with a <code>target.unsupported</code> diagnostic that points at the source.</li><li>Add the first target, <code>ros-gz-nav2</code>, composed of a Gazebo adapter (URDF links, joints and inertials from the analysis placements, meshes copied from the libraries, sphere wheel contacts with class-based friction, simulated laser, IMU and camera sensors, a ground-truth pose publisher, the world SDF), a ROS 2 adapter (<code>ros2_control</code> interfaces, the mecanum and differential drive controllers fed by the analysis kinematics, one <code>/cmd_vel</code><code>TwistStamped</code> entry point) and the composition (topic bridge, mission file, <code>bringup.launch.py</code>). Skid-steer, tricycle, Ackermann and omni bases are refused until a controller is bound and verified.</li><li>Add the output write phase to the driver: <code>--target NAME --output DIR</code> writes one directory, refuses an output that is, holds or sits inside a source or library, overwrites only its own files, and adds <code>compile.json</code> with the facts, validation results, approximations and provenance.</li><li>Add the in-image test tiers: <code>pytest -m container</code> checks the emitted files with the target&#39;s own tools, <code>pytest -m simulation</code> launches the directory and judges motion by the simulator&#39;s poses.</li><li>Add navigation and task execution to <code>ros-gz-nav2</code>: <code>nav/nav2.base.yaml</code> (the target&#39;s Nav2 policy) and <code>nav/nav2.robot.yaml</code> (footprint, inflation, the path follower for the base&#39;s kinematics, the laser the costmaps watch), <code>task/behavior_tree.xml</code> (the task in BehaviorTree.CPP layout over the vocabulary), and <code>runtime/</code> (a task executor that binds each node to Nav2 actions, <code>/cmd_vel</code>, odometry and TF, and writes <code>run/task_result.json</code>). The launch starts Nav2 after the drive controller and the task after Nav2, and ends when the task ends. The simulation tier runs the whole demo and checks the task result against the simulator&#39;s pose at the goal.</li><li>Execute the <code>.eval</code> file: the target emits <code>task/evaluation.json</code> (the checks and the planar collision shapes of parts and entities), the runtime records the simulator&#39;s pose and the joint states while the task runs, and <code>run/evaluation.json</code> gives three verdicts from independent evidence: the task&#39;s own status, the physical outcome from ground truth (goal reached within twice the controller&#39;s tolerance, no obstacle touched), and every check with the value it was judged on. The vocabulary states what each check&#39;s bounds mean; <code>Check_contact</code> does not count a support surface.</li><li>Draw the map from the world: every obstacle is rasterized into <code>nav/map.pgm</code> for the map server, the global costmap gets a static layer, and a robot with a laser localizes against the map with AMCL from its spawn pose. A robot without a laser keeps the map frame pinned to odometry.</li><li>Bound the vocabulary to what the compiler acts on: <code>Arm</code>, <code>Gripper</code> and <code>Tray</code> are one <code>Attachment</code> type, <code>Ultrasonic</code> is gone until a target simulates it, and an architecture test checks that every component type, task node, check and entity type has a consumer in analysis, validation or a target.</li><li>Support a depth camera: simulated in Gazebo, its image and cloud bridged, and, on a robot without a laser, its cloud projected onto the scan that the costmaps and AMCL read (<code>runtime/run_depth_scan.py</code>).</li><li>Require Python 3.12, the version CI and the target image already use; the runtime payload&#39;s numpy stubs need it for type checking.</li><li>Name a part&#39;s profiles after the component types: a library entry lists the types the part can be used as (<code>profiles: {Lidar: ...}</code>), and the separate lower-case profile names are gone. The vocabulary table is Type, Category, Meaning; the chassis table names chassis and wheel types. Facts, rules and diagnostics say &quot;chassis&quot; and &quot;wheel type&quot; where they said &quot;profile&quot; and &quot;class&quot; (<code>assembly.wheel_type_matches_chassis</code>, <code>name.type_not_supported</code>).</li><li>Drive a <code>Diff_drive</code> (four-wheel skid-steer) base: the differential controller takes both wheels of a side, and each wheel&#39;s sphere contact grips along the rolling direction and slides across it.</li><li>Drive tricycle and Ackermann bases: a chassis declares the kingpin of a steered mount (<code>steering: {axis, max_angle_rad}</code>), the analysis reports which wheels drive, steer or only roll and the base&#39;s minimum turning radius, a rule asks for an upright kingpin on every steered mount, the robot description hangs steered wheels from knuckle links, the tricycle and Ackermann steering controllers are bound, and such a base plans with Smac Hybrid-A* and follows with MPPI&#39;s Ackermann model. The launch takes <code>navigation:=false</code> to bring up only the simulator, the robot and its drive controller.</li><li>Sense with a spinning lidar (<code>Lidar3D</code>: a 16-ring <code>gpu_lidar</code> whose middle ring is bridged as a planar scan and whose cloud is bridged as points) and an RGBD camera (<code>RGBD</code>: colour and depth images and a cloud). Every point cloud is an observation source of both costmaps (points 0.05 to 2 m above the ground); the localizer reads the first laser, else the first spinning lidar&#39;s ring, else a scan projected from the first depth or RGBD camera&#39;s cloud.</li><li>Sense with an ultrasonic range finder (<code>Ultrasonic</code>): the simulator has no such sensor, so a <code>gpu_lidar</code> of 9 by 9 beams fills its cone and <code>runtime/run_range_sensor.py</code> reports the nearest return above the floor as a <code>sensor_msgs/msg/Range</code>, which the costmaps&#39; range layer marks. A robot with only range finders navigates on the known map from its spawn pose.</li><li>Sense with a bumper (<code>Bumper</code>): an ideal <code>contact</code> sensor on the part&#39;s collision box, bridged as <code>ros_gz_interfaces/msg/Contacts</code>. The task runtime records its reports, and <code>Check_contact</code> on a bumper is judged from them (seconds pressed, entities that pressed it) instead of from the geometry.</li><li>Drive omni bases (<code>Omni_three_wheel</code>, <code>Omni_drive</code>) in any layout: the analysis reads each wheel&#39;s position and rolling direction, the rule <code>kinematics.omni_wheels_span_the_plane</code> asks that the directions span the plane, each wheel&#39;s contact grips along its roll and slides along its axle, and the base is bound to a joint group velocity controller fed by <code>runtime/run_omni_drive.py</code>, which computes the wheel speeds and the odometry from the layout. ros2_controllers&#39; omni controller models only wheels evenly spaced on a circle; the catalog&#39;s four-wheel omni bases sit on rectangles with their axles along the diagonals.</li><li>Prepare the targets for a second simulator: the mission and evaluation data every runtime reads move to <code>targets/mission</code>, the runtime files every target copies are assembled by <code>targets/payload</code>, the robot description can be built without Gazebo&#39;s blocks, and the simulated sensors&#39; stand-in specifications are public.</li><li>A second target, <code>ros-mujoco-nav2</code>: MuJoCo simulates the robot through the runtime&#39;s own node (<code>runtime/run_mujoco_sim.py</code>, <code>world/mujoco.json</code>) and ros2_control&#39;s topic-based hardware; the robot description, controllers, navigation, task runtime and evaluation are the Gazebo target&#39;s. Lasers, spinning lidars and range finders cast rays, cameras render offscreen, the IMU and the bumper read the physics state. Bases on mecanum or omni wheels are refused: MuJoCo&#39;s contact friction is isotropic; for the same reason a skid-steer base turns at about a third of the commanded rate, which the target declares as an approximation. The test image is <code>robotdsl/ros-mujoco-nav2:jazzy</code> (<code>docker/ros-mujoco-nav2/jazzy</code>).</li><li>A statics rule, <code>statics.mass_inside_the_wheel_contacts</code>: the robot&#39;s centre of mass must fall strictly inside the polygon of its ground contacts, the wheels and the skids or balls a part declares under <code>contacts</code> (now placed into the facts), or the robot tips at rest. When the contacts lie on one line the rule is undecided and asks for the skid or a caster. Found when the demo two-wheel base, whose part declared its mass over the axle with nothing on the caster, tipped onto its nose under braking in both simulators and its depth camera saw the floor as a wall; the rule reports the margin, and points at the chassis with every contact.</li><li>Convert the previous compiler&#39;s schema-3 component library into this format with <code>tools/convert-library</code> (<code>mobiledsl.library.convert</code>): renamed fields, the joint moved onto the wheel&#39;s connector, profiles named after component types, meshes copied under <code>assets/</code>, and a report of what was dropped or left absent. The real catalog converts to 248 loadable parts out of 254; the six the loader refuses are gaps in the data (placeholder inertials without a basis, an id declared in two categories, a tricycle without a kingpin).</li><li>A defective part declaration no longer blocks every program that uses the library: the part is left out with a warning, and a program that names it gets a name-resolution error saying why it could not be loaded. Found when the converted catalog&#39;s four incomplete parts blocked robots built from the 248 complete ones.</li></ul>',9)])])}const p=t(n,[["render",r]]);export{m as __pageData,p as default};
